WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.000
I'm Jo Dunkley
and I'm here to tell you

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.840
about three amazing women astronomers

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.200
who, in the late 19th
and 20th Centuries,

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:13.040
faced down challenges many
of us would recognise today.

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.680
Despite the obstacles,
they each managed

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.120
to change the way
we understand the universe.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.200
Astronomer Henrietta Swan Leavitt

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.360
joined Harvard College
Observatory in 1895.

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:32.760
She was part of an extraordinary
group of women

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.440
known as the Harvard Computers.

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.800
Their job was to classify stars
using photographic images.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:46.560
As women, they weren't allowed
to operate the telescopes themselves

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:48.400
and were paid very little.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:53.280
Leavitt studied stars that get
brighter and dimmer over time.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.520
She discovered a pattern in 1908
now known as Leavitt's Law.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:00.080
Which says that...

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.800
This means that just by measuring
the rate of pulsing,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.800
which might be days or weeks,

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.680
and by seeing how bright
the star appears from Earth,

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:20.480
an astronomer can find out
how far away it is.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.440
This was transformational.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.160
Edwin Hubble used Leavitt's discovery
in the 1920s

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:31.480
to work out that smudges
of light in the sky

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.640
were in fact entire galaxies
far beyond our own.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:39.280
The universe was much bigger
than we thought.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:43.920
I have such admiration for Leavitt
because she was assigned

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.960
very mundane tasks in her job yet
still made this vital breakthrough.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:56.360
Cecilia Payne-Gaposchkin also worked
at Harvard College Observatory

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:58.080
after Swan Leavitt.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.520
In the early 1920s,
she used the latest learnings

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.000
about quantum physics
to come up with the idea

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:12.080
that stars are all made mainly
of hydrogen and helium gas.

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.000
Up until then, no one had known
what stars are made of.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:21.400
They were thought to have similar
ingredients to our planet, Earth.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.200
Payne-Gaposchkin said that stars are
much simpler than anyone thought.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:32.920
However, the well-known astronomer
Henry Norris Russell

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:38.840
advised her to remove her idea
from her PhD thesis in 1925

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.360
as it went against
the current wisdom.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.640
Just a few years later,

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:45.960
Payne-Gaposchkin
would be proved right.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:48.200
She would later go on to become

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:52.240
the first female head of astronomy
at Harvard University.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.760
I wonder if Payne-Gaposchkin
would have been discouraged

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:59.920
from her bold new idea
if she had been a man.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.640
Luckily it didn't stop her
from achieving great things.

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:11.280
Vera Rubin was
an American astronomer.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.840
She finished her studies in the 1950s

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.480
while juggling caring
for her young children,

00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.880
often having to attend lectures
in the evenings.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:25.880
Rubin wanted to study
how entire galaxies spin

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:27.760
and she became the first woman

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:31.440
allowed to use the
Palomar Observatory in California.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:35.240
At the time, there was
no women's bathroom.

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:40.080
But undeterred, Rubin pasted a paper
skirt on the men's bathroom door

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.480
to create her own.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.880
Rubin discovered
something surprising.

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.480
All of the galaxies she looked at
were spinning too fast,

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:55.560
as if something more massive
but completely invisible

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.160
was holding them together.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.720
Rubin showed that
every galaxy is surrounded

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:06.040
by a huge halo
of invisible dark matter.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:10.000
50 years later,
we still don't know what it is.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:13.440
Rubin's story inspires me.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.440
Her discovery is so important
to my current research,

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:22.040
and I relate to her as a mother
trying to balance doing science

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.880
with looking after young children.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:26.760
It's not easy
but it's a fun challenge.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.240
These women all made
great discoveries in astronomy,

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.080
and despite being little-known
throughout their lives,

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.680
their stories continue to inspire
women across the world.

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:45.900
If you enjoyed this video, subscribe to the BBC Ideas YouTube channel for more.

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.120
Thanks for watching.

